(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-axial-skeleton-quiz-version-4"]={"title":"The Axial Skeleton — Anatomy Exam 4 — Set 4","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"Which part of a typical vertebra bears weight and lies anteriorly?","choices":["The spinous process","The transverse process","The superior articular facet","The body (centrum)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vertebral body, or centrum, is the thick anterior weight-bearing portion of a typical vertebra. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"The vertebral foramen of a typical vertebra transmits which structure?","choices":["The spinal cord","The vertebral artery only","A spinal nerve root only","The esophagus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The vertebral foramen is the central opening through which the spinal cord passes; the stacked foramina form the vertebral canal. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"The vertebral arch is formed by which two paired structures?","choices":["The body and the spinous process","The transverse and spinous processes","The pedicles and the laminae","The superior and inferior bodies"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vertebral arch is formed by the paired pedicles anteriorly and the paired laminae posteriorly, enclosing the vertebral foramen. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which feature is unique to cervical vertebrae and transmits the vertebral artery?","choices":["The transverse foramina","The costal facets","The large oval body","The sacral hiatus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cervical vertebrae uniquely possess transverse foramina in their transverse processes, which transmit the vertebral arteries. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What is the distinguishing feature of the atlas (C1)?","choices":["It bears the dens (odontoid process)","It has the longest spinous process","It carries costal facets for ribs","It lacks a body and a spinous process"],"answer":3,"exp":"The atlas (C1) is a ring-like bone that lacks both a vertebral body and a true spinous process, supporting the skull. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which peg-like projection of the axis (C2) allows the head to rotate?","choices":["The mastoid process","The spinous process of C7","The transverse process of C1","The dens (odontoid process)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The dens, or odontoid process, projects upward from the axis (C2) and forms the pivot around which the atlas and head rotate. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Why is C7 called the vertebra prominens?","choices":["It bears the dens for skull rotation","Its long spinous process is easily felt","It lacks a vertebral body entirely","It is fused into the sacral bone"],"answer":1,"exp":"C7 is called the vertebra prominens because its long, non-bifid spinous process forms a prominent, easily palpable bump at the base of the neck. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which feature of thoracic vertebrae allows them to articulate with the ribs?","choices":["Transverse foramina in the processes","A bifid, two-pronged spinous process","Costal facets (demifacets) on the body","A completely missing vertebral body"],"answer":2,"exp":"Thoracic vertebrae bear costal facets, often as demifacets, on their bodies and transverse processes for articulation with the ribs. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which feature best distinguishes lumbar vertebrae from the others?","choices":["Transverse foramina in the transverse processes","Costal facets on the body for the rib heads","Large, thick bodies with short spinous processes","A thin ring shape with no vertebral body"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lumbar vertebrae have the largest, thickest bodies and short, blunt, posteriorly directed spinous processes to bear body weight. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"How many vertebrae fuse to form the sacrum?","choices":["3 fused vertebrae","5 fused vertebrae","7 fused vertebrae","12 fused vertebrae"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sacrum is formed by the fusion of 5 sacral vertebrae into a single triangular bone. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which anterior sacral ridge projects into the pelvic cavity at the sacrum's superior border?","choices":["The sacral promontory","The median sacral crest","The sacral hiatus","The coccygeal cornu"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sacral promontory is the anterior projecting ridge of the first sacral vertebra's body, an important pelvic landmark. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"The inferior opening at the end of the sacral canal, used for certain anesthetic injections, is called what?","choices":["The greater sciatic notch","The sacral hiatus","The obturator foramen","The intervertebral foramen"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sacral hiatus is the opening at the inferior end of the sacral canal, formed where the laminae of the fifth sacral vertebra fail to fuse. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"How many vertebrae typically fuse to form the coccyx?","choices":["Always exactly 7","Always exactly 12","Usually 4 (range 3 to 5)","Always exactly 5, never fewer"],"answer":2,"exp":"The coccyx usually forms from the fusion of 4 small coccygeal vertebrae, though the number ranges from 3 to 5. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which ribs are classified as true ribs, attaching to the sternum by their own costal cartilage?","choices":["Ribs 8 through 10","Ribs 11 and 12","Ribs 1 through 5","Ribs 1 through 7"],"answer":3,"exp":"True ribs are ribs 1 through 7, each attaching directly to the sternum by its own costal cartilage. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which ribs are the floating ribs, lacking any anterior attachment?","choices":["Ribs 1 and 2","Ribs 11 and 12","Ribs 8 through 10","Ribs 6 and 7"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ribs 11 and 12 are floating ribs; their anterior ends are free, with no attachment to the sternum or costal cartilage above. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Along the inferior inner surface of a typical rib, which groove protects the intercostal vessels and nerve?","choices":["The costal groove","The intertubercular groove","The radial groove","The mylohyoid groove"],"answer":0,"exp":"The costal groove runs along the inferior internal margin of a typical rib and shelters the intercostal vein, artery, and nerve. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which three regions make up the sternum from superior to inferior?","choices":["Manubrium, body, and xiphoid process","Head, neck, and shaft","Ala, promontory, and hiatus","Ramus, angle, and condyle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sternum consists of the manubrium superiorly, the body in the middle, and the xiphoid process inferiorly. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"The sternal angle (angle of Louis) marks the articulation of which rib with the sternum?","choices":["The first rib","The seventh rib","The second rib","The twelfth rib"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sternal angle, the ridge between the manubrium and body, marks the level where the second costal cartilage joins the sternum. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which superior notch of the manubrium is easily palpated at the base of the throat?","choices":["The mandibular notch of the jaw","The greater sciatic notch of the hip","The jugular (suprasternal) notch","The radial notch of the ulna"],"answer":2,"exp":"The jugular, or suprasternal, notch is the central depression on the superior manubrium, readily felt at the base of the throat. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which two spinal curves are the primary (fetal) curves, concave anteriorly?","choices":["The cervical and lumbar curves","The cervical and thoracic curves","The lumbar and sacral curves","The thoracic and sacral curves"],"answer":3,"exp":"The thoracic and sacral curves are the primary curves, present from fetal life and concave anteriorly. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which two spinal curves are the secondary curves that develop after birth?","choices":["The cervical and lumbar curves","The thoracic and sacral curves","The thoracic and lumbar curves","The cervical and sacral curves"],"answer":0,"exp":"The cervical and lumbar curves are the secondary curves, developing after birth as an infant holds up the head and begins to walk. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which structures together form the bony thoracic cage?","choices":["The ribs, sternum, and thoracic vertebrae","The clavicles, scapulae, and ribs","The sternum, hyoid, and clavicles","The lumbar vertebrae and pelvis"],"answer":0,"exp":"The thoracic cage is formed by the 12 pairs of ribs, the sternum anteriorly, and the 12 thoracic vertebrae posteriorly. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which midline bump on the external occipital bone is a palpable muscle-attachment landmark?","choices":["The sella turcica of the sphenoid","The crista galli of the ethmoid","The mental protuberance of the chin","The external occipital protuberance"],"answer":3,"exp":"The external occipital protuberance is the midline bump on the back of the occipital bone, palpable and serving as a muscle and ligament attachment. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which cranial fossa is the deepest and lodges the cerebellum and brainstem?","choices":["The anterior cranial fossa","The posterior cranial fossa","The middle cranial fossa","The pterygopalatine fossa"],"answer":1,"exp":"The posterior cranial fossa, formed largely by the occipital bone, is the deepest and houses the cerebellum and brainstem around the foramen magnum. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which air-filled space, the largest of the paranasal sinuses, lies within the maxilla?","choices":["The frontal sinus","The sphenoidal sinus","The maxillary sinus","The mastoid air cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"The maxillary sinus is the largest paranasal sinus, occupying the body of the maxilla on each side. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Between adjacent vertebral bodies, what structure acts as a cushioning pad?","choices":["The costal cartilage","The intervertebral disc","The nuchal ligament","The articular capsule of the hip"],"answer":1,"exp":"The intervertebral disc is the fibrocartilage pad between adjacent vertebral bodies that cushions and allows slight movement. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which paired facial bones form the prominences of the cheeks?","choices":["The nasal bones","The zygomatic bones","The lacrimal bones","The palatine bones"],"answer":1,"exp":"The zygomatic bones form the prominences of the cheeks and part of the lateral orbital walls. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"The frontal bone forms the forehead and the superior margin of which structures?","choices":["The nasal cavity floor","The orbits (eye sockets)","The foramen magnum","The mandibular fossa"],"answer":1,"exp":"The frontal bone forms the forehead and the superior rim (supraorbital margin) of the orbits. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Spinal nerves exit the vertebral column through which openings between adjacent vertebrae?","choices":["The vertebral foramina","The transverse foramina","The nutrient foramina","The intervertebral foramina"],"answer":3,"exp":"Spinal nerves pass out of the vertebral canal through the intervertebral foramina, the lateral openings between the pedicles of adjacent vertebrae. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which posterior part of a typical rib articulates with the bodies of the thoracic vertebrae?","choices":["The head of the rib","The costal cartilage","The costal groove","The anterior shaft"],"answer":0,"exp":"The head of the rib, at its posterior end, articulates with the bodies of the thoracic vertebrae at the costovertebral joints. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 98"}]};
